  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "A teacher instructs each student in her class to write down a different integer between 1 and 49. She claims that there will be at least one pair of students whose numbers sum to 50. How many students must be in her class for this claim to hold true?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p37-q12-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "26 students.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p37-q12-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use 24 pairs summing to 50 and the singleton 25 as 25 pigeonholes; 26 distinct numbers force a complete pair.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Partition the integers into 25 classes:\n$$\\{1,49\\},\\{2,48\\},\\ldots,\\{24,26\\},\\{25\\}.$$\nWith 26 students, the pigeonhole principle guarantees that two chosen numbers belong to the same class. Since the numbers are different, this cannot be the singleton $\\{25\\}$. Therefore, the two numbers sum to 50.\n\nWith only 25 students, the numbers $1,2,\\ldots,25$ could be chosen. No two different numbers in this selection sum to 50. Hence the minimum is $26$ students.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
